Kino.Download.new(fn -> Chapter07.Store.encode(result_actor.genotype) end,
  filename: "ch_07_actor_champion.etf", label: "Download actor-trained champion genotype")
```

The versioned download contains a genotype, not an entire resumable trainer.
Reproducing or resuming an experiment additionally needs its random tape or random
state, configuration, counters, and relevant scape state. Only load checkpoints
from trusted sources; `binary_to_term(..., [:safe])` is not a complete defense
against arbitrary hostile files.

### Experiment 6 - Operational failure is not low fitness

Terminate one neuron, request evaluation, and require an operational error. Then
close the whole phenotype. The supervisor may log the intentional termination.
This is a failure-detection and cleanup check, not automatic recovery.

```elixir
tbl_failure_checks = Chapter07.Checks.failure(genotype_template)
Chapter07.Tables.tabs(tbl_failure_checks, [:check, :result], "ch_07_tbl_10_failure_checks.csv")
```

## 7.8 Adding the benchmarker Module

*Source: [S], pp. 223-226; Listing 7.11.*

A benchmarker repeats the entire trainer, collects fitness, evaluations, cycles,
and elapsed time, and reports minimum, maximum, mean, and standard deviation.
We retain the source's **population standard deviation** with denominator $n$:

$$
\bar x=\frac{1}{n}\sum_i x_i,
\qquad
\sigma=\sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_i(x_i-\bar x)^2}.
$$

The printed listing calls `std/1` but defines an `avg_std/1` entry point instead.
Our implementation supplies one consistent `statistics/1` function. It rejects
empty samples and gives zero standard deviation for a single value.

**Remaster reporting rule:** budget-exhausted runs stay in the results. We also
report how many runs reached the target. Mean evaluations across all runs is
**mean consumed budget**, not mean evaluations-to-solution when some runs failed.
The source's historical result table is not reproduced as a result of this code.

```elixir
defmodule Chapter07.Benchmarker do
  alias Chapter07.{Actor, Config, Genotype, Hybrid, Layout, Tape, Tensor, Trainer}
  @moduledoc "Repeated, bounded trials. Budget exhaustion remains in the reported sample."

  @spec run(:actor | :tensor | :hybrid, Genotype.t(), Tape.t(), Config.t()) :: map()
  def run(:actor, template, tape, config), do: Trainer.run(Actor, template, tape, config)
  def run(:tensor, template, tape, config), do: Tensor.run(template, tape, config)
  def run(:hybrid, template, tape, config), do: Trainer.run(Hybrid, template, tape, config)

  @spec repeat(Genotype.t(), [non_neg_integer()], Config.t()) :: [map()]
  def repeat(template, seeds, config) do
    unless seeds != [], do: raise(ArgumentError, "at least one seed is required")
    layout = Layout.new(template)
    Enum.flat_map(Enum.with_index(seeds), fn {seed, index} ->
      tape = Tape.new(layout, config.evaluation_limit, seed)
      # Rotate execution order to avoid always running one implementation first.
      order = [:actor, :tensor, :hybrid]
      {left, right} = Enum.split(order, rem(index, 3))
      Enum.map(right ++ left, fn implementation ->
        result = run(implementation, template, tape, config)
        %{implementation: implementation, seed: seed, fitness: result.fitness,
          success: result.fitness >= config.fitness_target, evaluations: result.evaluations,
          cycles: result.cycles, restarts: result.restarts, elapsed_ms: result.elapsed_ms,
          reason: result.reason}
      end)
    end)
  end

  @spec statistics([number()]) :: map()
  def statistics(values) do
    unless values != [], do: raise(ArgumentError, "cannot summarize an empty sample")
    count = length(values)
    mean = Enum.sum(values) / count
    variance = Enum.reduce(values, 0.0, fn x, sum -> sum + (x - mean) * (x - mean) end) / count
    %{mean: mean, std: :math.sqrt(variance), min: Enum.min(values), max: Enum.max(values)}
  end

  @spec summarize([map()]) :: [map()]
  def summarize(rows) do
    rows |> Enum.group_by(& &1.implementation) |> Enum.sort_by(&elem(&1, 0))
    |> Enum.flat_map(fn {implementation, group} ->
      successes = Enum.count(group, & &1.success)
      Enum.map([:fitness, :evaluations, :cycles, :elapsed_ms], fn metric ->
        stats = statistics(Enum.map(group, &Map.fetch!(&1, metric)))
        Map.merge(stats, %{implementation: implementation, metric: metric, runs: length(group),
          successes: successes, success_rate: successes / length(group)})
      end)
    end)
  end
end
```

### Experiment 7 - Repeat bounded searches with paired seeds

The default below uses three seeds and a 600-evaluation budget to keep the notebook
bounded. Increase the number of seeds for a substantive study. The source's
example used 100 trials with a target and no evaluation limit; that is a different
experimental protocol.

The implementation order rotates by seed. All methods for a seed use the same
tape. Timings include setup, conversions, and any compilation/tracing incurred by
the call. They are not normalized CPU/GPU kernel timings. In particular,
BinaryBackend measurements cannot establish accelerator speedups.

```elixir
benchmark_config = Chapter07.Config.new(evaluation_limit: 600, local_patience: 80,
  restart_patience: 5, fitness_target: 100.0)
benchmark_seeds = [11, 22, 33]
tbl_benchmark_runs = Chapter07.Benchmarker.repeat(genotype_template, benchmark_seeds, benchmark_config)
Chapter07.Tables.tabs(tbl_benchmark_runs,
  [:implementation, :seed, :fitness, :success, :evaluations, :cycles, :restarts, :elapsed_ms, :reason],
  "ch_07_tbl_11_benchmark_runs.csv")
```

```elixir
tbl_benchmark_summary = Chapter07.Benchmarker.summarize(tbl_benchmark_runs)
Chapter07.Tables.tabs(tbl_benchmark_summary,
  [:implementation, :metric, :runs, :successes, :success_rate, :mean, :std, :min, :max],
  "ch_07_tbl_12_benchmark_summary.csv")
```

```elixir
fig_benchmark = Vl.new(width: 680, height: 280, title: "R7.5 - Consumed evaluations, including budget exhaustion")
  |> Vl.data_from_values(tbl_benchmark_runs)
  |> Vl.mark(:point, filled: true, size: 90)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:x, "implementation", type: :nominal)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:y, "evaluations", type: :quantitative)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:color, "success", type: :nominal)
  |> Vl.encode_field(:tooltip, "seed", type: :nominal)
fig_benchmark
```

For a fair architecture study, hold the genotype, observations, fitness definition,
random input, and budgets constant. Separate cold-start timing from warmed
execution when that distinction matters. If strict decisions diverge, inspect the
first differing evaluation before interpreting a timing or convergence difference.

## 7.9 Summary

*Source: [S], pp. 226-227.*

We now have a fixed-topology network that can be optimized by an external process,
a standard task interface, random restarts with a global champion, and repeated
experiments with recorded statistics. In Sher's terminology this is **external
training**, not the lifetime neural plasticity discussed earlier.

| Thesis | Antithesis | Synthesis |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Individual neural processes hold parameters and exchange signals | A tensor state machine holds parameters and performs the complete search | A network actor owns tensor parameters while outside tasks manage search |
| Scape feedback closes each action cycle | A batch evaluates XOR's independent cases | A worker evaluates the same batch and returns episode-level feedback |
| Backup/restore are acknowledged process operations | Rejection retains the previous tensor | Backup/restore switch between accepted and proposed tensor values |
| Failure is detected at runtime process boundaries | No application-level actor lifecycle in the numerical core | Failure boundaries are coarser than one neuron |

**GES remains the algorithmic framework.** Generation proposes weights, Evaluation
measures performance through the scape, and Selection decides acceptance and
archive replacement. Thesis-Antithesis-Synthesis remains the implementation and
comparison framework. Neither framework implies that the hybrid must win.

### Final checks

These aggregate the earlier checks and validate the benchmark accounting. They
intentionally do not assert a particular random run's success, a speed ranking,
or exact equality of long floating-point search histories.

```elixir
stats_example = Chapter07.Benchmarker.statistics([1.0, 2.0, 3.0])
checks_benchmark = [
  Chapter07.Support.check!(abs(stats_example.mean - 2.0) < 1.0e-12, "Benchmark mean"),
  Chapter07.Support.check!(abs(stats_example.std - :math.sqrt(2.0 / 3.0)) < 1.0e-12,
    "Benchmark SD uses the source's denominator N"),
  Chapter07.Support.check!(Chapter07.Benchmarker.statistics([7.0]).std == 0.0,
    "Single-run population SD is zero"),
  Chapter07.Support.check!(length(tbl_benchmark_runs) == 3 * length(benchmark_seeds),
    "No bounded benchmark runs were omitted"),
  Chapter07.Support.check!(Enum.all?(tbl_benchmark_runs, fn row ->
    row.cycles == 4 * row.evaluations and row.evaluations <= benchmark_config.evaluation_limit
  end), "Every benchmark run respects cycle and evaluation accounting")
]
tbl_all_checks = tbl_contract_checks ++ tbl_search_checks ++ tbl_failure_checks ++ checks_benchmark
Chapter07.Tables.tabs(tbl_all_checks, [:check, :result], "ch_07_tbl_13_all_checks.csv")
```

### Exercises

| Exercise | Change | What to inspect |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Patience versus total work | Change `local_patience` while keeping the total evaluation budget fixed | Number of starts, accepted improvements, and target success rate |
| Mutation granularity | Inspect neuron and parameter gates separately | Correlated changes within a neuron; no-op proposals |
| Strict versus neutral acceptance | Build a separately labeled variant that accepts ties | Plateaus, failure counters, and changed search semantics |
| Error definition | Add a separately named MSE evaluator to all three backends | Recalibrate the target; do not reuse the printed-score target blindly |
| Batching boundary | Replace XOR with a task whose next observation depends on the action | Which operations must remain sequential? |
| Runtime granularity | Compare per-neuron and per-network workers | Messaging, conversion, lifecycle, and failure boundaries |

Keep the default `[2]` fixture when running the supplied fixed-size fixture and
scripted-policy checks. To study another hidden topology, construct it with
`Morphology.construct/3`, create a matching `Layout` and tape, and use the general
trainer/tensor interfaces; adapt the hard-coded fixture tests separately.

### Source-to-remaster decisions

| Source location | What the source contains | Explicit remaster treatment |
| --- | --- | --- |
| pp. 188-190 | Local patience, restart patience, random starts, masked perturbation | Preserve the two levels; use clear zero-based consecutive-failure counters |
| p. 190 | Local patience proportional to square root of network size | Expose a parameter; 80 is a notebook choice, not a printed multiplier |
| pp. 203-204 | ETS with tuple record keys | Store `{id, map}` entries; close temporary tables explicitly |
| pp. 206-207 | Trainer startup and loop arities disagree in the printed listing | Supply a consistent function/task interface |
| pp. 208-209 | Terminal evaluation count and explanatory variable names are inconsistent | Count every completed evaluation; reset failure counters, not total evaluations |
| p. 212 | A per-case square root followed by another square root; called MSE | Preserve the executable score and name it printed/source fitness |
| p. 214 | `EFAcc` versus `HFAcc`; misspelled `termiante` | Use one halt path and supervised teardown |
| pp. 216-218 | Current/backup weights and scalar bias handling | Represent bias explicitly, preserve mutation eligibility and denominator |
| p. 219 | Zero-vector substitution for invalid sensor width | Abort the evaluation rather than score fabricated input |
| pp. 220-222 | Mixed error thresholds, one reported run, discussion of saturation | Derive thresholds from the actual score; label the fixture and avoid a universal maximum claim |
| pp. 224-225 | `std/1` calls versus `avg_std/1` definition; variance denominator N | One checked statistics function with the same denominator |
| Across the chapter | Process-local randomness, global registered names, unbounded defaults | Recorded draws, explicit handles, bounded reproducible experiments |
| Across the comparison | No Nx implementation in the original | Clearly labeled tensor and hybrid remaster additions |

An acknowledged runtime backup is not a durable checkpoint. A genotype export is
not a complete training-session checkpoint. A supervisor detecting a crash is not
a repaired neural graph. These distinctions remain important in later chapters.

### References

**[S]** Gene I. Sher. *Handbook of Neuroevolution Through Erlang*. Springer, 2013.
Chapter 7, "Adding the Stochastic Hill-Climber Learning Algorithm," pp. 187-227.
DOI: `10.1007/978-1-4614-4463-3_7`. The uploaded 41-page chapter is the basis for
its terminology, section sequence, algorithm, interfaces, and source audit.

The software references below support the **new implementation**, not Sher's
2013 account. Dependency versions remain pinned to the earlier notebooks.

| Key | Official implementation reference | Used for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| D1 | [Nx](https://nx.hexdocs.pm/Nx.html) and [Nx.Defn](https://nx.hexdocs.pm/Nx.Defn.html) | Tensor shapes, numerical definitions, backends |
| D2 | [Nx.Defn.Kernel](https://nx.hexdocs.pm/Nx.Defn.Kernel.html) | Fixed-shape loop state and compile-time transforms |
| D3 | [Erlang ETS](https://www.erlang.org/doc/apps/stdlib/ets.html) | Table ownership and file persistence |
| D4 | [GenServer](https://hexdocs.pm/elixir/GenServer.html) | Client/server callbacks and regular messages |
| D5 | [Task.Supervisor](https://hexdocs.pm/elixir/Task.Supervisor.html) | Explicitly supervised task lifetimes |
| D6 | [Kino.Layout](https://hexdocs.pm/kino/Kino.Layout.html) and [Kino.Download](https://hexdocs.pm/kino/Kino.Download.html) | Tabbed computed tables and binary downloads |

